15. Formerly vendored: TestSamples/FastWaveBackend — REMOVED 2026-07-31
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

This section is retained as a record. The directory it describes is no
longer in the working tree; it remains in git history (see below).

What it was:

  Location:  `TestSamples/FastWaveBackend/` — 55 tracked files, ~39 MB
  Upstream:  https://git.joyofhardware.com/Yehowshua/FastWaveBackend
  Copyright: Copyright (2023) Yehowshua Immanuel

FastWaveBackend is the Rust VCD-parsing backend that preceded `wellen` as
the engine behind the Surfer waveform viewer; its own README records that
work on it stopped in January 2024 in favour of `wellen`. It entered this
repository as an orphaned git submodule (gitlink `bbe5435f`, with no
matching `.gitmodules` entry) and was flattened into tracked files by
commit `d26b8c21`, "chore: add FastWaveBackend test samples".

The contents were TWO separable things under DIFFERENT terms — an earlier
revision of this file wrongly described the whole directory as GPL-3.0:

  1. FastWaveBackend's own Rust source (~140 KB; `src/`, `examples/`,
     `tests/*.rs`, `Cargo.toml`) — genuinely GPL-3.0, with an additional
     grant to the author in `LICENSE-YEHOWSHUA`.

  2. A ~36 MB VCD corpus (33 files under `tests/vcd-files/`) spanning 17
     simulators — NOT FastWaveBackend's work to license. Its own
     `tests/vcd-files/sources.csv` recorded that every trace but one was
     harvested from a different third-party GitHub repository (~40 repos
     in total); only the Amaranth file was locally simulated. Those files
     therefore carried NO stated license — a weaker position than a plain
     GPL conflict, because there was nothing to comply with.

Why it was removed rather than kept:

  - Nothing in the repository referenced it. No Dart, YAML, shell, TOML,
    Cargo, or CI file named it; it was in no Cargo workspace, was never
    built or linked, and contributed nothing to any artifact. Three
    documents mentioned it prose-only; those were updated in the same
    commit.
  - It conflicted with this project's own written fixture policy.
    `test/fixtures/real_world/README.md` states "GPL/AGPL/proprietary
    files are excluded by policy" and requires every committed fixture to
    carry a `.provenance.json` sidecar naming an allow-listed license
    (MIT, BSD-2/3-Clause, Apache-2.0, ISC, CC0-1.0, public domain). The
    static guardrail `test/static/real_world_fixture_provenance_test.dart`
    enforces exactly that — but is scoped to
    `const _zooDir = 'test/fixtures/real_world'`, which is why this
    directory survived it.
  - The coverage was already rebuilt properly. The `real_world/` zoo
    spans the same vendor matrix (VCS, Xcelium/ncsim, Verilator, Icarus,
    Aldec, GTKWave, GHDL, cocotb, Yosys) with full provenance sidecars.

Note on history: `git rm` removes the files from the working tree, not
from prior commits. The GPL-3.0 source and the unlicensed corpus remain
reachable in this repository's history at and before `d26b8c21`. If the
open-source flip requires that they not be present in the published
history at all, that needs a history rewrite (e.g. `git filter-repo`),
which is a separate and more disruptive operation. See §16.4.


